117 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Charge-Coupled Devices \(CCDs\) are silicon-based semiconductor detectors widely used in soft X-ray astronomy, particularly in the 0.1 to 10 keV energy range. When an incoming X-ray photon strikes the CCD, it deposits energy into the silicon, generating a number of electron-hole pairs proportional to the photon's energy. These charges are collected and stored in discrete potential wells that form a pixel array. After exposure, the charges are shifted across the chip and read out sequentially. This process enables precise measurements of both the position and energy of each photon, allowing for high-resolution imaging and moderate spectral resolution. To reduce thermal noise and improve sensitivity, CCDs are typically cooled to very low temperatures.)
/E (Charge-Coupled Devices \(CCDs\) are silicon-based semiconductor detectors widely used in soft X-ray astronomy, particularly in the 0.1 to 10 keV energy range. When an incoming X-ray photon strikes the CCD, it deposits energy into the silicon, generating a number of electron-hole pairs proportional to the photon's energy. These charges are collected and stored in discrete potential wells that form a pixel array. After exposure, the charges are shifted across the chip and read out sequentially. This process enables precise measurements of both the position and energy of each photon, allowing for high-resolution imaging and moderate spectral resolution. To reduce thermal noise and improve sensitivity, CCDs are typically cooled to very low temperatures.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [1]
>>
